What Are Rack-Based Coolant Distribution Units?
Rack-based CDUs are cooling management systems installed within or alongside server racks to regulate coolant flow through liquid-cooling loops. They help maintain stable operating temperatures, protect sensitive computing equipment, and support reliable operation under demanding workloads.

These systems are particularly important for AI servers, GPU-intensive computing, cloud infrastructure, and other applications with high thermal requirements. Product selection depends on cooling capacity, rack density, facility architecture, coolant compatibility, monitoring capabilities, and integration with existing data center infrastructure.

Key Market Drivers
Growing Demand for AI and High-Performance Computing
The expansion of generative AI, machine learning, and advanced analytics is increasing demand for high-performance servers. These systems can generate substantial heat during intensive workloads, encouraging operators to adopt liquid cooling to maintain performance and reliability.

Rack-based CDUs provide localized coolant management and support high-density computing deployments, making them an important component of modern AI infrastructure.

Increasing Data Center Investments
Cloud computing, digital services, enterprise applications, and hyperscale infrastructure continue to drive data center development. As operators expand server capacity, they are evaluating cooling architectures that can accommodate higher rack power densities without excessive energy consumption or space requirements.

Transition from Air Cooling to Liquid Cooling
Traditional air cooling becomes more challenging as computing equipment becomes denser and thermal loads increase. Liquid cooling transfers heat more efficiently and can support demanding applications that exceed the practical capabilities of conventional air-based systems.

This transition is encouraging investment in rack-level cooling equipment, including CDUs designed for modular deployment and integration with existing facilities.

